      "latency_ms": 2711.7,
      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 678,
      "output": "At its most basic level, **water** is a chemical compound made of two elements: **hydrogen** and **oxygen**. Its chemical formula is **$\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$**, meaning each molecule of water contains two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom. \n\nHowever, to understand water truly, you have to look at it through a few different lenses: its physical properties, its role on Earth, and its importance to life.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Physical and Chemical Properties\nWater is famous for being uniquely \"weird\" compared to other substances on Earth:\n* **The Three States:** Water is the only natural substance on Earth that exists abundantly in three physical states: **liquid** (water), **solid** (ice), and **gas** (steam or water vapor).\n* **The Universal Solvent:** Because of its chemical structure, water can dissolve more substances than any other liquid. This is why it tastes like things (minerals, sugars) and why it can transport nutrients through blood streams and plant stems.\n* **Density Anomaly:** Most substances shrink and become denser when they freeze. Water, however, *expands* and becomes less dense when it turns into ice. This is why ice floats. If ice sank, lakes and oceans would freeze from the bottom up, destroying aquatic life.\n* **High Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb a lot of heat before its temperature changes significantly. This helps regulate the climate of the entire planet and keeps living organisms from overheating.\n\n### 2. Water on Earth\nWater is the defining feature of our home planet, often called the \"Blue Planet.\"\n* **Abundance:** About **71% of the Earth’s surface** is covered by water, mostly in oceans and seas.\n* **Distribution:** Roughly **96.5%** of Earth's water is saltwater in the oceans. Only **2.5%** is freshwater, and most of that is locked away in glaciers, ice caps, or buried deep underground. Less than 1% of Earth's water is easily accessible for human use in lakes, rivers, and soil.\n* **The Water Cycle:** Water is constantly recycled through the Earth in a closed loop driven by the sun. It evaporates from oceans and lakes, forms clouds (condensation), falls back to Earth as rain or snow (precipitation), and flows back to the oceans (collection).\n\n### 3. The Essence of Life\nBiologically speaking, water *is* life. \n* **Inside Us:** The human body is made of about **60% water**. The brain is about 73% water, the lungs about 83%, and even bones are about 31% water. \n* **Cellular Fuel:** Every cell in every living organism—from bacteria to blue whales—uses water as a medium to carry out chemical reactions, transport nutrients, and remove waste.\n* **Evolution:** Scientists believe life itself originated in Earth's primordial oceans around 3.8 billion years ago, relying on water's unique ability to host complex chemical reactions.\n\n### Summary\nIn short, water is a simple molecule with extraordinary properties. It shapes our geography, drives our weather, and serves as the fundamental building block for all known life."
